{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Switch Statement in Java"}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when describing switch statements in Java."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Talk about principles, concepts, and characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Imperatives"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the <strong>imperative</strong> to give instructions, advice or interdictions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The imperative is a direct mode, so you don't need to use a subject pronoun.</br> <p>&nbsp;</br><strong>Affirmative structure</strong></br> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Main verb in base form + complement</strong></br> <p>For example:</br> <ul> <li><i><strong>Type&nbsp;</strong>a code in the switch statement.</i></li> <li><i><strong>Skip</strong>&nbsp;that command.</i></li> </ul> <br><p><strong>Negative structure</strong></br> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Auxiliary DO + not + main verb in base form + complement</strong></br> <p>&nbsp;</br> For example:&nbsp;</br> <ul> <li><i><strong>Do not type</strong> a code in the switch statement!</i></li> <li><i><strong>Don&rsquo;t skip</strong> that command!</i></li> </ul>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Think about a command and type it. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Next time, do not worry about the math function but about the structure of the statement. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Imagine that the program finds a different number that doesn't agree with the code."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Have Got to and Going to Have to for What Is Necessary"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use<i> Have got to</i> and<i> Going to have to</i> to express the necessity to perform a particular action."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p><strong>Have got to</strong><br />It has the same meaning of&nbsp;<strong>have to</strong>; however, it is a little bit more formal.<br />To make statements with&nbsp;<strong>have got to</strong>&nbsp;you follow this structure:<br /> <br />S +&nbsp;<strong>Have/Has</strong>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<strong>Got to</strong>&nbsp;+ Main verb in base form + Complement<br /> <br />Examples:&nbsp;<br /> <br />&middot; <i>We</i><i>&nbsp;</i><strong><i>have got to</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>work with switch statements.</i><br />&middot; <i>The engineer</i><i>&nbsp;</i><strong><i>has got to</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>check the values.</i><br /> <br />As you can see, you conjugate &ldquo;have got to&rdquo; with the subject pronouns (I, you, we and they) and &ldquo;has got to&rdquo; with the subject pronouns (He, She and It).</br> <p><br /> <br /> <strong>Going to have to</strong></br> <p>In the case of&nbsp;<strong>going to have to</strong>, it is quite similar to&nbsp;<strong>going to need to</strong>.<br />To make statements with&nbsp;<strong>going to have to</strong>&nbsp;you follow this structure:&nbsp;<br /> <br />S + To be verb +&nbsp;<strong>going to have to</strong>&nbsp;+ Main verb in base form + Complement<br /> <br />Examples:&nbsp;<br /> <br />&middot; <i>We</i><i>&nbsp;</i><strong><i>are going to have to</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>work with switch statements.</i><br />&middot; <i>The engineer</i><i>&nbsp;</i><strong><i>is going to have to</i></strong><i>&nbsp;</i><i>check the values.</i><br /> <br />As you can see, you conjugate the verb&nbsp;<strong>be</strong>&nbsp;depending on the subject pronoun. But &ldquo;going to have to&rdquo; remains the same.&nbsp;</br>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "This command has got to go to a position in the switch statement."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Imagine a code where you have got to register a particular number to perform a task. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "These go-to positions are also known as case labels, which are going to have to emit constant values that have got to be the same as switch expressions. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "What the program is going to have to do is to skip the default function and stop immediately."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "speed up "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to accelerate a process"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: These are control mechanisms that branch a program and speed the decision-making process up."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "type "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to write on a computer's keyboard"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Think about a command and type it."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob10aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "label "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a tag that classifies something depending on its particular characteristics"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: These go-to positions are also known as case labels."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob10aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "emit "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to send out, to transmit"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Case labels are going to have to emit constant values that have got to be the same as switch expressions."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob10aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "match "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to agree or coordinate with something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In case these ones don't match, there is a default keyword at the end of the statement."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob10aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "fallout "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an unexpected consequence"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The default keyword captures this fallout and avoids an invalid input in the program execution."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob10aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Design a switch statement for any program you want. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write a text explaining the statement and how you created it. Use the imperatives to describe it, and the \u201chave to\u201d and \u201cgoing to have\u201d. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share with your partners."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I use the switch statement and explain how to set it.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I explain what the switch statement is. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I design and explain how to design a switch statement."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob10re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "Computer programming languages, such as Java, have got to work with switch statements. These are control mechanisms that branch a program and speed the decision-making process up. For instance, think about a command and type it. Once you've done that, the command will be placed in a fixed position in the switch statement. These go-to positions are also known as case labels, which are going to have to emit constant values that have got to be the same as switch expressions. In case these ones don't match, there is a default keyword at the end of the statement that captures this fallout and avoids an invalid input in the program execution."}, {"time": "38500", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob10re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "On the other hand, knowing how to use the syntax of a switch statement is mandatory.  It consists of patterns that establish the different commands and codes to execute a function. The syntax of switch statements is considered very effective since it allows the program to find expressions in an accurate way, without checking statement by statement. So, next time, do not worry about the math function but about the structure of the statement. And remember that sometimes break statements are necessary to help the program to skip the switch statement ending."}, {"time": "70000", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob10re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "To conclude, take a look at these examples. Imagine a code where you have got to register a particular number to perform a task. Now, imagine that the program finds a different number that doesn't agree with the code. What the program is going to have to do is to skip the default function and stop immediately. Now, think about a game. Once you start playing it, the computer is going to have to determine certain paths within the switch statements depending on your choices."}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le02ob10re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n5le02ob10re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "title": ""}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can say what the switch statement is in Java.  "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about the syntax of a switch statement in Java.   "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can discuss examples of switch statements in Java."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Why do you think syntax is important in Java?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What do you know about the syntax of switch statements in Java?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "All About Switch Statements in Java!"}], "id": 0, "type": "WLC", "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "img_path": ""}, {"stimuli_ids": [1], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " Do you know what is a switch statement?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Content"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "conten